      "id": "holdout-late-conclusion",
      "source_type": "YOUTUBE",
      "source_language": "en",
      "response_language": "en",
      "content": "A three-month performance review tested whether the dashboard cache should move to a read replica. The review covered the dashboard service, its query path, and the nightly ETL schedule. Engineers recorded query plans before each change and kept the schema fixed for the whole period. During the first two weeks they measured cache hit rate, lock waits, and request volume on each replica. The workload stayed between 1,100 and 1,400 requests per minute, with a predictable peak after the nightly load finished. Two earlier attempts were rejected: adding a second writer increased contention, and shortening the cache TTL raised database load without improving latency. The team then moved only the read path. The review covered the dashboard service, its query path, and the nightly ETL schedule. Engineers recorded query plans before each change and kept the schema fixed for the whole period. During the first two weeks they measured cache hit rate, lock waits, and request volume on each replica. The workload stayed between 1,100 and 1,400 requests per minute, with a predictable peak after the nightly load finished. Two earlier attempts were rejected: adding a second writer increased contention, and shortening the cache TTL raised database load without improving latency. The team then moved only the read path. The review covered the dashboard service, its query path, and the nightly ETL schedule. Engineers recorded query plans before each change and kept the schema fixed for the whole period. During the first two weeks they measured cache hit rate, lock waits, and request volume on each replica. The workload stayed between 1,100 and 1,400 requests per minute, with a predictable peak after the nightly load finished. Two earlier attempts were rejected: adding a second writer increased contention, and shortening the cache TTL raised database load without improving latency. The team then moved only the read path. Final result: moving the dashboard cache to a read replica cut median dashboard load time from 4.8 s to 1.9 s after 10:00, but before 10:00 the gain disappeared because the nightly ETL job still held the lock. The team kept the replica only for post-ETL traffic and did not claim a gain during the ETL window.",
